= Human-computer interaction — Technical =

== Human–system loop ==
An interaction can be modeled as:

<pre>
user goal
→ action
→ system state change
→ feedback/perception
→ updated user model
→ next action
</pre>

Breakdowns can occur at any translation.

== Performance models ==
HCI uses models such as Fitts' law, Hick–Hyman law, GOMS-style task models, information-foraging models and cognitive architectures.

Each abstracts a restricted aspect of behavior.

== Evaluation validity ==
Laboratory experiments provide control; field studies provide ecological context.

Telemetry provides scale but limited explanation.

Interviews expose experience but depend on report and interpretation.

Method triangulation can strengthen inference.

== Accessibility and inclusive design ==
Technical accessibility includes semantic structure, keyboard access, contrast, alternatives to sensory modalities, assistive-technology compatibility and robust interaction states.

== Automation ==
Mixed-initiative systems introduce questions of control, explainability, error recovery, trust calibration and appropriate reliance.

